<i>- Hey, hey</i>
<i>- Hey, hey</i>

153
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:20,749
<i>- Red Beret</i>
<i>- Red Beret</i>

154
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:22,194
<i>- Second Squad</i>
<i>- Second Squad</i>

155
00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:24,316
<i>- Lead the way</i>
<i>- Lead the way</i>

156
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:29,312
Soldier, you're in deep trouble.

157
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:31,550
I went off the base for the night.
I'm back now.

158
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:33,676
Lieutenant Walters
was murdered last night.

159
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:35,273
- Murdered?
- Stabbed to death

160
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:37,112
in the officer's club with this.

161
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:39,954
- I found it in your footlocker.
- Wait a minute. Wait a minute.

162
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,475
He was alive when I left last night.
You saw me.

163
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:45,359
Somebody else saw you there
20 minutes later,

164
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:47,636
after everybody
but the lieutenant had left.

165
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:50,792
- That's a lie.
- You save it for the court-martial.

166
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:53,874
Place him in custody.

167
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,592
Ben.

168
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:53,753
Ben.

169
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:55,428
Ben.

170
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:59,396
- Arthur.
- Ben, I have a problem.

171
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:00,754
Don't yell, Arthur.

172
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:03,480
I know I should've waited till tomorrow
and come to your office.

173
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:05,949
That's right, Arthur.
I don't talk business on Sunday.

174
00:14:06,120 --> 00:14:07,519
This is an emergency.

175
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:09,796
- I don't care, Arthur.
- It's about Chris.

176
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:12,713
- I heard.
- You've gotta help him, Ben.

177
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:15,155
I have to be in Germany tomorrow
to close a deal

178
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:16,992
I've been working on for two years.

179
00:14:17,160 --> 00:14:18,513
Now, I need your help.

180
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:20,079
I spoke to Chris a little while ago

181
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:22,800
and I told him that you'd be down
to talk to him right away.

182
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:26,919
It's not right for you to come driving
out here and stomping up on my porch

183
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:29,719
and telling me that you promised Chris
I'd be down there to see him

184
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:32,110
in a little while. You want some
of this chicken sandwich?

185
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:35,033
- Will you listen to me?
- You've always been that way, Arthur.

186
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:37,270
They have him on a murder charge.

187
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:40,238
As long as I can remember,
you've come stomping up on my porch

188
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:41,833
and telling me what I have to do.

189
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,195
Now, I'm working on other cases
right now.

190
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:45,869
Postpone them.

191
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:48,429
Anytime you want something
or you're in some kind of trouble,

192
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:50,556
you expect everybody
to drop everything.

193
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:53,712
The Army will appoint him a lawyer.

194
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:57,116
Chris has got an attitude problem.

195
00:14:57,280 --> 00:14:59,510
I was hoping the Army
was going to knock that out of him.

196
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:01,671
I don't remember Chris
having an attitude problem.

197
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:04,195
I remember you always had
an attitude problem.

198
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:07,034
I don't think he's got a prayer
without someone like you.

199
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:09,156
- Arthur.
- Please.

200
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:13,711
The Army doesn't like civilians
messing in their stuff.

201
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:16,990
Look, Ben, why don't we
just cut through all this?

202
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:21,836
I know that your standard
rich-client fee is $1 00,000.

203
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,913
I am going to write a cheque
for 1 50,000.

204
00:15:28,720 --> 00:15:30,358
Two hundred thousand?

205
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:35,475
Two hundred and fifty
thousand dollars.

206
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:39,637
I'll go down and see Chris.

207
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:42,314
Make out the cheque for $1 00,000.

208
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:46,551
How much it's going to cost you
when it's all over

209
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:49,029
depends on how mad I get at you.

210
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:11,275
Tell me about the night of the murder.

211
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:14,558
Well...

212
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:20,039
When they pronounced Bobby
Thomas dead--

213
00:16:20,200 --> 00:16:23,351
He was a young man that
the lieutenant killed on manoeuvres.

214
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:28,069
--well, I was so angry
I went down to the officers' club

215
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:30,071
and I tried to get
into a fight with him.

216
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,479
Who else was there?

217
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:35,874
The lieutenant was there

218
00:16:36,040 --> 00:16:39,396
with Colonel McRea,
Captain Campbell,

219
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:42,438
Major Hamilton,
and Lieutenant Lynch.

220
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:43,828
They were playing poker.

221
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:48,990
- See anybody else?
- No. Just the bartender.

222
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:51,230
- It was late.
- Then you left.

223
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:53,789
- Yeah.
- Where'd you go when you left?

224
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:56,713
Then I drove around
for a couple hours.

225
00:16:56,880 --> 00:16:58,552
I finally wound up at Nancy's.

226
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:01,678
She's this girl I know in town.
I spent the rest of the night there.

227
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:03,398
So there's no way you can prove

228
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,551
where you were
at the time of the murder.

229
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:07,189
No.

230
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:10,869
This bayonet they found
in your footlocker,

231
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:14,078
- any idea who put it there?
- No.

232
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:17,391
The fella who says he saw you
come back to the club,

233
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:19,357
any idea why he'd lie?

234
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:21,353
No idea.

235
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:24,638
Not much to go on, is there?

236
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:26,434
Why'd you come down here?

237
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:28,552
Chris,

238
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:31,509
I take cases for three reasons.

239
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:34,399
Sometimes I'll take a case for money
and I don't think that's wrong.

240
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:37,518
It doesn't bother me.
That's the way I make my living.

241
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:42,037
Sometimes I take one because
clearly an injustice is being done.

242
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:44,589
Sometimes I'll take one
because it interests me.

243
00:17:45,160 --> 00:17:46,878
But somewhere in my mind,

244
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:51,556
I must believe
the person I represent is innocent.

245
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:53,039
What about me?

246
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:56,756
I think an injustice is being done
and the case interests me.

247
00:17:56,920 --> 00:17:58,478
That answer any of your questions?

248
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:00,473
Yeah.

249
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:03,030
Let's say we get together
and do this.

250
00:18:03,880 --> 00:18:05,836
Yeah. Okay.

251
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,752
I better go or I'll be late
for my appointment

252
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:09,114
with the base commander.

253
00:18:09,280 --> 00:18:11,555
- Sergeant.
- Mr. Matlock,

254
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:13,517
what do you think my chances are?

255
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:15,750
Oh, there's always
a fighting chance.

256
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,309
The truth has a way of coming out.

257
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:20,755
See if I can get you out of here.

258
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:36,917
Go left, go left, go left, right, left.

259
00:18:37,080 --> 00:18:40,356
Go left, go left, go left, right...

260
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:44,433
Go left, go left, go left, right, left.

261
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:48,559
Go left, go left, go left, right, left.

262
00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:51,678
Go left, go left...

263
00:18:52,400 --> 00:18:54,197
Morning, sir.

264
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:57,918
Morning, sir.

265
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:04,118
Morning, sir.

266
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:11,916
Excuse me, son.

267
00:19:12,680 --> 00:19:13,715
Yes, sir.

268
00:19:14,240 --> 00:19:16,834
- I seem to be lost.
- How can I help you, sir?

269
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:19,715
I'm looking for
General Connors' office.

270
00:19:19,880 --> 00:19:22,599
Headquarters is two blocks up
the road, second door on the right, sir.

271
00:19:22,760 --> 00:19:25,558
- Thank you.
- Anytime sir.

272
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:41,352
Arthur Hughes and I
go back to OCS.

273
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:44,193
He left the Army
after about three years.

274
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:45,475
I decided to stay.

275
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:47,876
And you kept in touch all this time.

276
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:49,189
Mr. Matlock,

277
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:52,473
this is awkward for me.
I hope you understand my position.

278
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:54,474
Not exactly.

279
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:58,557
Well, an investigating officer
whom I've already appointed

280
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:02,395
will be conducting
an Article 32 investigation.

281
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:05,632
That's a hearing where testimony and
evidence are presented to the officer,

282
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:08,473
and then he, in turn,
presents his hearings to me.

283
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:11,190
Well, it's been up to me to determine

284
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:13,828
whether or not a court-martial
is warranted.

285
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:17,676
Well, I'm concerned about Chris.

286
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:19,831
How can I get him out
of the stockade?

287
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:22,119
Well, that's not so simple.

288
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:28,637
Would you ask Major Orlando
to step in here, please?

289
00:20:29,600 --> 00:20:30,953
He's our staff judge advocate.

290
00:20:31,120 --> 00:20:33,475
You'll have to discuss
Private Hughes' release with him.

291
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:36,393
- I'd be delighted.
- Don't be too sure.

292
00:20:37,240 --> 00:20:38,639
Yeah.

293
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:41,719
Major Orlando, Benjamin Matlock.

294
00:20:41,880 --> 00:20:44,030
Mr. Matlock
is Private Hughes' attorney.

295
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:45,792
He'd like him released
from the stockade.

296
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:47,279
That is not possible, sir.

297
00:20:47,440 --> 00:20:50,716
I'm not asking that he be freed,
just released from the stockade

298
00:20:50,880 --> 00:20:53,155
and placed under
some kind of house arrest.

299
00:20:53,320 --> 00:20:56,471
The evidence against
Private Hughes is overwhelming.

300
00:20:56,640 --> 00:20:57,993
You think he's guilty?

301
00:20:58,160 --> 00:21:00,116
What I meant to say is that,

302
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:03,033
well, Private Hughes
has already gone AWOL once.

303
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:05,430
What's to prevent him
from doing it again?

304
00:21:05,600 --> 00:21:06,919
With a base full of soldiers?

305
00:21:07,080 --> 00:21:09,036
Surely somebody
can keep an eye on him.

306
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:13,552
Release Private Hughes from the
stockade and confine him to quarters.

307
00:21:14,040 --> 00:21:15,109
Yes, sir.

308
00:21:15,760 --> 00:21:16,829
Anything else?

309
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,231
I'd like to take a look at the scene of
the crime, if it's not too much trouble.

310
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:24,076
I'll have the provost marshal meet you
at the officer's club ASAP.

311
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:27,869
Fine. And then I'd like to go through
all of that overwhelming evidence.

312
00:21:28,040 --> 00:21:29,758
Anytime.

313
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:47,039
- Morning, sir.
- Morning, sir.

314
00:22:00,960 --> 00:22:02,313
Morning.

315
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:04,796
Morning, what can I get for you?

316
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:07,838
How about a cup of coffee
and a little information?

317
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:09,399
What kind of information?

318
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:12,269
Well, this is my first day on the post.

319
00:22:12,440 --> 00:22:15,750
- I didn't enlist. I'm a civilian.
- Makes two of us.

320
00:22:16,520 --> 00:22:19,717
Everywhere I go, these young fellas
salute and call me sir.

321
00:22:19,880 --> 00:22:22,269
I might get to like that if I knew why.

322
00:22:22,440 --> 00:22:24,715
They get a lot of visitors around here,
VIP types.

323
00:22:25,760 --> 00:22:29,355
The boys see somebody like you,
you know, white hair and everything,

324
00:22:29,520 --> 00:22:32,512
think you're some kind of bigshot,
like a congressman or maybe a senator.

325
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:35,796
I figured it was my suit.

326
00:22:36,160 --> 00:22:37,354
- Mr. Matlock?
- Yes.

327
00:22:37,520 --> 00:22:39,636
Major Jeffrey Hamilton,
provost marshal.

328
00:22:39,800 --> 00:22:42,030
If you'll follow me,
I'll show you the scene of the crime.

329
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:43,428
Yes, sir.

330
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:55,033
Excuse my ignorance, major,
but what's a provost marshal?

331
00:22:56,080 --> 00:23:00,278
- Head MP.
- Oh, kind of like the chief of police.

332
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:02,517
I see.

333
00:23:08,840 --> 00:23:11,400
I understand you were here
the night of the murder.

334
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:15,033
That's right. Playing poker.

335
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:17,470
What time did the game break up?

336
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:19,949
About 1 0:45 or so.

337
00:23:21,640 --> 00:23:25,349
Well, why didn't Lieutenant Walters

338
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:27,910
leave with the rest of you?

339
00:23:28,080 --> 00:23:31,311
He stayed behind
to run some totals.

340
00:23:31,480 --> 00:23:34,119
He kept a book
on what everyone owed.

341
00:23:34,280 --> 00:23:35,793
I'd like to see that.

342
00:23:35,960 --> 00:23:37,632
- See what?
- The book.

343
00:23:37,800 --> 00:23:40,473
Is it with the rest
of the evidence or what?

344
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:43,517
Actually, it was never found.

345
00:23:46,440 --> 00:23:48,351
You mean the killer took it?

346
00:23:49,800 --> 00:23:52,268
Well, why would Chris Hughes

347
00:23:53,240 --> 00:23:57,199
be concerned with the accounts
of a poker game?

348
00:23:57,360 --> 00:24:00,318
I have no idea.
Why don't you ask him?

349
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:02,436
How do you suppose he knew

350
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:04,830
Lieutenant Walters
was in here by himself?

351
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,116
Maybe he waited for him outside

352
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:09,108
and just got lucky.

353
00:24:10,200 --> 00:24:11,474
Maybe.

354
00:24:12,600 --> 00:24:15,672
But then we know
there were several people

355
00:24:15,840 --> 00:24:17,159
who knew positively

356
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:19,629
that Lieutenant Walters
was in here alone, don't we?

357
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:23,197
One, two, three, four people.

358
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:25,430
Are you suggesting
that one of us killed him?

359
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:27,511
No, no, no.

360
00:24:27,680 --> 00:24:30,114
You were obviously friends,
playing cards like that.

361
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:35,557
I'd like my investigator
to look around in here.

362
00:24:35,920 --> 00:24:38,115
Our forensics people
have already gone over this room

363
00:24:38,280 --> 00:24:39,474
with a fine-toothed comb.

364
00:24:39,640 --> 00:24:41,073
We'll send you a copy of the report.

365
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,196
Now, if there's nothing--

366
00:24:43,360 --> 00:24:46,591
It's customary for the defence
to have the opportunity

367
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:48,637
to examine the scene of the crime.

368
00:24:49,080 --> 00:24:50,479
You just did.

369
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:53,750
I just did.

370
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:01,156
Where'd you go
after the poker game?

371
00:25:01,320 --> 00:25:02,833
Back to my office,

372
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:04,908
till midnight,

373
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:07,071
catching up on some paperwork.

374
00:25:07,720 --> 00:25:11,952
This Sergeant Owens who said he saw
Chris come back to the club that night,

375
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:13,394
where would I find him?

376
00:25:13,720 --> 00:25:16,473
- He works in post supply.
-Where is that?

377
00:25:17,520 --> 00:25:19,988
You go out the front door, turn left,

378
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:22,116
three blocks, left.

379
00:25:22,280 --> 00:25:23,554
It'll be right in front of you.

380
00:25:45,240 --> 00:25:47,435
- Sergeant Owens?
- Yes, sir.

381
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:49,113
My name's Ben Matlock.
I'm an attorney.

382
00:25:49,280 --> 00:25:50,599
I'd like to ask some questions.

383
00:25:51,720 --> 00:25:52,709
About what?

384
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:55,713
About what you saw the night
Lieutenant Walters was murdered.

385
00:25:55,880 --> 00:25:57,996
Well, I'm sorry, sir,
but I got a lot of work to do.

386
00:25:58,160 --> 00:26:01,197
Couldn't you work and talk
at the same time?

387
00:26:02,600 --> 00:26:04,556
Sergeant?

388
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:07,273
Hey, Sergeant.

389
00:26:07,440 --> 00:26:09,237
Wait a minute!

390
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:22,878
Oh, no. No way. Absolutely not.

391
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:24,598
Not in a million years.

392
00:26:24,760 --> 00:26:26,193
Out of the question.

393
00:26:26,360 --> 00:26:28,476
Tyler, let me put it this way:

394
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:29,629
You have to.

395
00:26:30,080 --> 00:26:32,150
Ben, I was in the Army once.
Once is enough.

396
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:34,038
What do you mean I have to?

397
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:37,829
Arthur Hughes and General Connors
had to go all the way to the Pentagon

398
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:39,638
to get you in Chris' platoon.

399
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:45,591
And as of now, you are in the Army,
back on active duty.

400
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:47,591
Ben, how could you do this to me?

401
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:48,988
I couldn't do it to her.

402
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:55,477
Chris said he was with a girl
named Nancy Lamont

403
00:26:55,640 --> 00:26:57,198
when he went AWOL. Find her.

404
00:26:57,360 --> 00:26:58,759
Consider it done.

405
00:26:59,640 --> 00:27:01,437
Good luck, Tyler.

406
00:27:03,480 --> 00:27:04,799
Tyler,

407
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:08,239
everybody on that post treats me
like I've got the plague.

408
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:11,597
Either that or I'm a member
of the first family.

409
00:27:11,760 --> 00:27:14,558
Now, your going undercover
is the only way

410
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:16,756
we're gonna be able
to investigate this thing.

411
00:27:16,920 --> 00:27:19,753
But as a private, Ben?
I was an officer.

412
00:27:19,920 --> 00:27:21,956
Do you realise
what you're asking of me?

413
00:27:23,360 --> 00:27:25,954
How soon can you be packed?

414
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:30,357
One, two, three, four.
Left, left, left, right, left.

415
00:27:30,520 --> 00:27:34,274
Wake up that guy, will you?
Left, left, left, right, left.

416
00:27:34,440 --> 00:27:36,954
One, two, three, four, left, right, left.

417
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:39,680
Left, left, left, right, left...

418
00:27:56,360 --> 00:27:58,874
Oh, boy, oh, boy, oh, boy.

419
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:03,671
Yo, where is everybody?

420
00:28:03,840 --> 00:28:05,717
Firing range. You the new man?

421
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:07,871
- Looks that way.
- Your name?

422
00:28:08,040 --> 00:28:10,713
- Hudson, Tyler.
- Rank.

423
00:28:12,040 --> 00:28:13,155
- Private.
- Private?

424
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:15,880
I thought you were a general
the way you're lounging around here.

425
00:28:16,040 --> 00:28:18,918
I'm Sergeant Wilson.
You get your butt into fatigues.

426
00:28:19,080 --> 00:28:22,311
I'm gonna make you
my special project, private.

427
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:30,555
Morning.

428
00:28:31,280 --> 00:28:34,431
Hey, you didn't tell me
you were that lawyer Ben Matlock.

429
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:36,033
- Yeah.
- My name is Danny Miller.

430
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:37,428
What can I get for you?

431
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:39,431
- A root beer?
- Yeah.

432
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:40,794
Danny,

433
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:43,915
you know, my grandfather
was a bartender

434
00:28:44,080 --> 00:28:46,514
in the days of buffalo and Indians.

435
00:28:46,680 --> 00:28:48,477
At a trading post, actually.

436
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:52,076
They didn't have newspapers,
but my grandfather knew everything.

437
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:54,800
Anybody wanted to know anything,
they'd ask my grandfather.

438
00:28:56,080 --> 00:28:58,196
Some things never change.

439
00:28:58,360 --> 00:29:01,079
- You can quote me on that.
- Maybe I will.

440
00:29:01,880 --> 00:29:04,952
What do you know about
the poker game in the back room?

441
00:29:07,040 --> 00:29:08,758
I'll tell you what I don't know:

442
00:29:08,920 --> 00:29:11,150
why five men who
couldn't stand each other's guts

443
00:29:11,320 --> 00:29:13,311
insisted on playing poker so often.

444
00:29:15,360 --> 00:29:18,238
Could you just be more specific?

445
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:24,869
Here you go.

446
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:26,359
It's diet.

447
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:28,351
Not that you need it,
but it's all I've got.

448
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:29,999
Oh, thanks.

449
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:33,792
So, what can I do for you?

450
00:29:33,960 --> 00:29:37,396
Well, Chris says he was here the night
Lieutenant Walters was murdered.

451
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:38,879
What time did he arrive?

452
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:41,319
Sometime after midnight.

453
00:29:41,480 --> 00:29:43,311
I had just gotten off work at the bar.

454
00:29:43,480 --> 00:29:46,836
And how was he when he got here?
Was he nervous or upset?

455
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,878
Chris didn't kill Lieutenant Walters.
He couldn't do something like that.

456
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:52,675
He tried to attack Lieutenant Walters
with a bayonet.

457
00:29:52,840 --> 00:29:53,955
Did you know that?

458
00:29:54,120 --> 00:29:57,954
No, I didn't, but I can tell you this:
Lieutenant Walters was a creep.

459
00:29:58,800 --> 00:30:00,791
Walters used to hang out
at your bar?

460
00:30:00,960 --> 00:30:02,393
Only on Saturday nights.

461
00:30:02,560 --> 00:30:04,471
He'd meet these two guys
and leave right away.

462
00:30:04,640 --> 00:30:05,629
What guys?

463
00:30:06,400 --> 00:30:07,879
Two civilians.

464
00:30:08,040 --> 00:30:09,792
Looked like a couple of hoods.

465
00:30:09,960 --> 00:30:12,030
And it was the same two guys
every Saturday night?

466
00:30:12,200 --> 00:30:13,428
Yeah.

467
00:30:13,600 --> 00:30:15,750
Maybe that means something.

468
00:30:15,920 --> 00:30:17,751
Not easy to get along with.

469
00:30:19,160 --> 00:30:22,357
Well, there he is now.
That's Colonel McRea.

470
00:30:27,720 --> 00:30:31,190
Just remember, Mr. Matlock,
you heard it anyplace but here.

471
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:34,716
Danny, take a lock.

472
00:30:37,320 --> 00:30:39,993
Colonel McRea? Benjamin Matlock.

473
00:30:40,160 --> 00:30:41,991
Glad you could make it
on such short notice.

474
00:30:42,160 --> 00:30:44,628
I never turn down a free lunch.

475
00:30:46,720 --> 00:30:48,551
Gentlemen?

476
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:50,233
Yeah.

477
00:30:53,320 --> 00:30:54,912
I wanted to ask you

478
00:30:55,080 --> 00:30:57,878
about the poker games you used
to play with Lieutenant Walters.

479
00:30:58,040 --> 00:31:01,316
Just an innocent little game
of five-card draw,

480
00:31:01,480 --> 00:31:04,517
jacks or better, $5 ante.

481
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:06,429
Did you win?

482
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:08,429
Sometimes.

483
00:31:11,240 --> 00:31:13,879
Did you like playing
with Lieutenant Walters?

484
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:15,473
Sure.

485
00:31:15,640 --> 00:31:18,234
Even though he made
a practise of insulting you?

486
00:31:18,400 --> 00:31:20,709
Walters kidded me sometimes.

487
00:31:20,880 --> 00:31:22,074
That's all.

488
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:24,435
Was he kidding
when he said things like

489
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:27,478
you're old, over the hill,
couldn't cut it anymore,

490
00:31:27,640 --> 00:31:30,279
couldn't walk and chew gum
at the same time,

491
00:31:30,440 --> 00:31:32,590
had this empty space
between your ears?

492
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:34,113
What are you driving at?

493
00:31:34,280 --> 00:31:37,511
I just wonder why you'd let
a junior officer treat you that way.

494
00:31:37,680 --> 00:31:39,238
He had a big mouth.

495
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:43,590
But he was okay underneath.

496
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:49,598
Where did you go
after the poker game

497
00:31:49,760 --> 00:31:51,876
the night Lieutenant Walters
was killed?

498
00:31:52,040 --> 00:31:54,474
Back to the supply depot.
I had some work to finish up.

499
00:31:54,640 --> 00:31:56,198
Anybody else there?

500
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:58,715
After I relieved Sergeant Owens, no.

501
00:31:59,760 --> 00:32:02,911
Is that the same Sergeant Owens
who said he saw Private Hughes

502
00:32:03,080 --> 00:32:04,308
come out of the officers' club?

503
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:07,911
He's my duty sergeant.

504
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:10,799
You know,
that's an interesting coincidence.

505
00:32:10,960 --> 00:32:16,034
If Sergeant Owens hadn't happened
to be passing by the club that night,

506
00:32:16,200 --> 00:32:20,159
there'd be no way to tie Chris Hughes
to the scene of the murder.

507
00:32:21,280 --> 00:32:23,271
Are you gentlemen
ready to order yet?

508
00:32:23,440 --> 00:32:27,877
I've already eaten,
but I wanna take care of the colonel.

509
00:32:28,480 --> 00:32:29,469
Enjoy your free lunch.

510
00:32:36,840 --> 00:32:39,638
- Afternoon, sir.
- Afternoon, sir.

511
00:32:39,800 --> 00:32:41,472
Good afternoon, sir.

512
00:32:51,720 --> 00:32:53,915
Oh, I apologise.

513
00:32:54,080 --> 00:32:55,672
You mind if I look around?

514
00:32:55,840 --> 00:32:58,559
- You see two stars on my shoulder?
- No.

515
00:32:58,720 --> 00:33:01,598
Well, you gotta outrank a brigadier
general to say no to you around here,

516
00:33:01,760 --> 00:33:03,113
so why don't you just be my guest?

517
00:33:03,280 --> 00:33:04,349
Thanks.

518
00:33:04,520 --> 00:33:07,398
Which one of these
is Chris Hughes' bunk?

519
00:33:23,680 --> 00:33:25,238
These footlockers always unlocked?

520
00:33:25,400 --> 00:33:27,118
That's right.

521
00:33:29,880 --> 00:33:32,394
- Mind if I...?
- Go ahead.

522
00:33:46,160 --> 00:33:48,469
I knew I'd find you
around here someplace.

523
00:33:49,280 --> 00:33:50,918
Don't talk to me.

524
00:33:51,360 --> 00:33:53,999
We can bust this case
against Chris Hughes wide open

525
00:33:54,160 --> 00:33:57,197
if we can only find out
where this Sergeant Owens really was

526
00:33:57,360 --> 00:33:58,713
at the time of the murder.

527
00:33:58,880 --> 00:34:00,711
I am not listening.

528
00:34:00,880 --> 00:34:03,314
He works at the supply depot
run by Colonel McRea.

529
00:34:03,480 --> 00:34:04,959
Talk to his coworkers.

530
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:07,509
Maybe one of them knows
where he was that night.

531
00:34:08,600 --> 00:34:11,068
Ben, do you see
what I'm doing here?

532
00:34:11,240 --> 00:34:13,037
Now, when am I supposed
to do this talking?

533
00:34:13,200 --> 00:34:14,633
I ask you, when?

534
00:34:16,760 --> 00:34:18,318
Tyler,

535
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:20,471
I know how unpleasant this is,

536
00:34:20,640 --> 00:34:23,871
and I can't tell you how much
I appreciate what you're doing.

537
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:26,231
And somehow I'll make it up to you.

538
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:30,234
I don't know how yet, but I will.
I swear.

539
00:34:33,160 --> 00:34:34,229
What are you looking at?

540
00:34:34,400 --> 00:34:35,674
You're going to be mad.

541
00:34:35,840 --> 00:34:36,989
Try me.

542
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:38,635
Well,

543
00:34:39,560 --> 00:34:40,834
you missed a spot.

544
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:45,233
I told you. I knew you'd be mad.

545
00:34:58,320 --> 00:35:00,709
- Here you go.
- Terrific.

546
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:03,314
- You look great in my clothes.
- Thanks.

547
00:35:03,480 --> 00:35:05,232
A little snug.

548
00:35:05,760 --> 00:35:08,593
You'd have stuck out like a sore thumb
if you'd worn that dress in here.

549
00:35:09,080 --> 00:35:10,308
I know.

550
00:35:10,480 --> 00:35:11,595
So...

551
00:35:13,080 --> 00:35:14,308
See that guy at the bar?

552
00:35:14,800 --> 00:35:16,074
His name is Jim Lynch.

553
00:35:16,240 --> 00:35:18,959
He's a lieutenant,
comes in here a lot.

554
00:35:19,360 --> 00:35:21,828
Every time those two guys
I told you about showed up,

555
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:24,070
he'd leave with them
just like Lieutenant Walters did.

556
00:35:24,240 --> 00:35:26,151
He's one of the men
Walters played poker with.

557
00:35:26,720 --> 00:35:28,836
Looks kind of Ionely to me.

558
00:35:34,640 --> 00:35:35,709
Is this seat taken?

559
00:35:35,880 --> 00:35:37,472
No, it's all yours.

560
00:35:38,120 --> 00:35:39,599
Thank you.

561
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:41,830
Have some pretzels.

562
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:44,833
Oh, actually, I'd like one of those.

563
00:35:45,640 --> 00:35:49,030
Lee? Look, could we have
a beer for the lady, please?

564
00:35:50,200 --> 00:35:51,269
You're a regular.

565
00:35:51,440 --> 00:35:53,510
I come in here once in a while.

566
00:35:53,680 --> 00:35:56,035
Often enough to know a new face
when I see one.

567
00:35:56,480 --> 00:35:59,870
Oh, I'm just visiting
an old friend from school.

568
00:36:00,040 --> 00:36:01,632
- Are you?
- Thanks.

569
00:36:01,800 --> 00:36:03,677
What's your excuse?

570
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:06,192
The Army.

571
00:36:06,360 --> 00:36:08,112
Oh, the Army.

572
00:36:13,240 --> 00:36:14,229
Seen the world?

573
00:36:15,480 --> 00:36:17,152
Just Fort Simpson

574
00:36:17,320 --> 00:36:18,548
and Grenada.

575
00:36:18,720 --> 00:36:20,676
You've seen actual combat.

576
00:36:20,840 --> 00:36:23,957
Yes. Yes, I have
actually seen combat.

577
00:36:26,840 --> 00:36:28,671
- Being all you can be?
- Yeah.

578
00:36:29,800 --> 00:36:31,279
Yes.

579
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:38,111
Look, I'm very sorry.
You'll have to excuse me.

580
00:36:38,280 --> 00:36:39,838
I have to go now,

581
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:42,036
but I'll see you around.

582
00:36:53,720 --> 00:36:54,914
<i>That's them.</i>

583
00:37:07,080 --> 00:37:11,392
They were gone about half an hour
and then Jim Lynch came back alone.

584
00:37:11,560 --> 00:37:13,073
What did you do?

585
00:37:13,240 --> 00:37:16,198
I spent another hour
talking with him.

586
00:37:16,360 --> 00:37:19,033
I told him I was in town visiting
a girlfriend who works at the bar,

587
00:37:19,200 --> 00:37:20,758
not Nancy.

588
00:37:21,880 --> 00:37:23,677
These two fellas
that left with Jim Lynch,

589
00:37:23,840 --> 00:37:25,717
what do you know about them?

590
00:37:25,880 --> 00:37:28,952
Well, they're not big socialisers,
but they sure are big spenders.

591
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:31,554
Good, good.

592
00:37:31,720 --> 00:37:33,472
You did good.

593
00:37:33,880 --> 00:37:36,110
- You better go home now.
- No, you go home. I'm staying.

594
00:37:36,280 --> 00:37:39,317
No, you better go home. We don't know
what we're getting into here, Charlene.

595
00:37:39,480 --> 00:37:41,277
Well, don't you think
we'd better find out?

596
00:37:41,440 --> 00:37:44,989
Dad, I might be able to get
something more out of this lieutenant.

597
00:37:45,160 --> 00:37:48,436
Maybe if I talk with him
he'll let something slip.

598
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:49,953
Nancy will put me up.

599
00:37:52,120 --> 00:37:54,998
Besides, her clothes even fit.

600
00:37:55,640 --> 00:37:58,757
That Article 32 investigation
starts tomorrow.

601
00:37:59,600 --> 00:38:03,388
I want you to be rested,
confident, cooperative.

602
00:38:03,560 --> 00:38:04,879
You didn't do it,

603
00:38:05,040 --> 00:38:07,110
and that's the way
I want you to look.

604
00:38:07,280 --> 00:38:08,679
Are you making any progress?

605
00:38:08,840 --> 00:38:11,195
- Some.
- Like what?

606
00:38:11,360 --> 00:38:12,713
You have to be patient.

607
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:14,472
In other words, nothing.

608
00:38:17,160 --> 00:38:18,957
That's just great.

609
00:38:21,280 --> 00:38:23,510
You're in a difficult position.

610
00:38:23,680 --> 00:38:25,830
There are a lot of unknowns,

611
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:29,231
but you have to know
that I have been here before,

612
00:38:29,400 --> 00:38:30,674
many times.

613
00:38:31,360 --> 00:38:33,157
I am doing my best

614
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:36,153
and I will do my very best.

615
00:38:36,320 --> 00:38:38,311
You have to trust me on that.

616
00:38:57,960 --> 00:38:59,154
- Company?
- Platoon.

617
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:00,878
Fall out.

618
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:04,874
Captain?

619
00:39:05,040 --> 00:39:06,553
Captain Campbell?

620
00:39:06,720 --> 00:39:07,948
May I have a word with you?

621
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:09,792
My wife is gonna pick me up
just about now.

622
00:39:09,960 --> 00:39:11,075
This will only take a moment.

623
00:39:11,240 --> 00:39:14,118
I just wanted to ask you some questions
about the formal complaints

624
00:39:14,280 --> 00:39:15,918
that were filed against
Lieutenant Walters

625
00:39:16,080 --> 00:39:18,230
- while he was under your command.
- Such as?

626
00:39:18,400 --> 00:39:21,995
Well, on one occasion, he supposedly
slapped a soldier across the face,

627
00:39:22,160 --> 00:39:24,754
and on another, he made a man
stand at attention in the sun

628
00:39:24,920 --> 00:39:26,592
for five hours till he passed out.

629
00:39:26,760 --> 00:39:29,797
Those complaints were thoroughly
investigated, Mr. Matlock.

630
00:39:29,960 --> 00:39:32,599
Why was there no investigation
into Bobby Thomas' death?

631
00:39:32,760 --> 00:39:33,749
There will be.

632
00:39:33,920 --> 00:39:36,514
It seems to me as Lieutenant Walters'
commanding officer,

633
00:39:36,680 --> 00:39:39,911
you should have been questioning him
instead of playing poker with him.

634
00:39:40,080 --> 00:39:42,469
As a matter of fact, I think nothing
came of those complaints

635
00:39:42,640 --> 00:39:44,312
because somebody
was looking out for him,

636
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:46,994
- kind of keeping him out of trouble.
- You mean me?

637
00:39:47,160 --> 00:39:50,596
- Sooner or later, the complaints had--
- I resent what you are insinuating.

638
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:52,712
You're right.

639
00:39:52,880 --> 00:39:54,552
You're right,
it doesn't make any sense.

640
00:39:54,720 --> 00:39:56,392
Why cover up for Lieutenant Walters

641
00:39:56,560 --> 00:40:00,633
when everybody knows
he and your wife were good friends?

642
00:40:01,080 --> 00:40:02,195
What are you talking about?

643
00:40:02,360 --> 00:40:04,555
Oh, I'm sorry. I thought you knew.

644
00:40:04,720 --> 00:40:05,948
It's all over the post.

645
00:40:06,120 --> 00:40:08,270
Bill, I was waiting for you outside.

646
00:40:08,440 --> 00:40:10,271
Hello. I'm Ben Matlock.
I was just leaving.

647
00:40:12,760 --> 00:40:13,954
Bill?

648
00:40:15,080 --> 00:40:17,640
Like they say, three is a crowd.

649
00:40:39,920 --> 00:40:41,751
Find out anything?

650
00:40:43,320 --> 00:40:46,153
Someone who is positive
that they saw Sergeant Owens,

651
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:49,153
your alleged eyewitness,

652
00:40:49,320 --> 00:40:51,276
in the supply depot

653
00:40:51,440 --> 00:40:54,034
and not at the officers' club
at the time of the murder.

654
00:40:54,200 --> 00:40:56,634
Tyler, I knew you could do it.
What's his name?

655
00:40:56,800 --> 00:40:58,597
His name is Parker.

656
00:40:58,760 --> 00:41:02,036
He's a sergeant.
Works in the mess as a cook.

657
00:41:02,200 --> 00:41:03,952
Now, he told somebody

658
00:41:04,120 --> 00:41:05,633
who told somebody.

659
00:41:05,800 --> 00:41:07,518
Now I am telling you.

660
00:41:08,760 --> 00:41:12,878
Ben, please,
when can I get out of here?

661
00:41:23,640 --> 00:41:27,838
How can you be sure you saw
Sergeant Owens that particular night?

662
00:41:28,000 --> 00:41:29,718
Well, I was working late

663
00:41:29,880 --> 00:41:32,155
and I remember that
because I almost never work late.

664
00:41:32,320 --> 00:41:35,437
And I remember it was
a little bit after 1 1 ,

665
00:41:35,600 --> 00:41:37,158
because that's the time I got off.

666
00:41:37,320 --> 00:41:39,515
And I remember
walking past the depot

667
00:41:39,680 --> 00:41:41,432
and seeing Owens
all by himself and thinking:

668
00:41:41,600 --> 00:41:44,353
''What do you know? Somebody else
is worse off than me for a change.''

669
00:41:45,120 --> 00:41:48,635
Sergeant Parker,
you're just what the doctor ordered.

670
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:50,836
Hearing's tomorrow, 9:00.
See you then.

671
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:52,558
Right.

672
00:42:05,080 --> 00:42:07,913
- Morning, Major Orlando.
- Hello, Mr. Matlock.

673
00:42:08,080 --> 00:42:10,196
That's a heavy-Iooking briefcase.

674
00:42:10,360 --> 00:42:12,271
I hope it's not all evidence
against my client.

675
00:42:12,440 --> 00:42:13,839
I believe in being well-prepared,

676
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,833
even in cases as open-and-shut
as this one.

677
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:24,759
- Well, morning, Colonel.
- Good morning, Mr. Matlock.

678
00:42:24,920 --> 00:42:25,989
- Major Orlando.
- General.

679
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:27,513
Yes?

680
00:42:27,680 --> 00:42:30,399
- Did you get a telegram?
- No.

681
00:42:31,200 --> 00:42:32,679
Yeah, well, I did.

682
00:42:32,840 --> 00:42:34,273
From the old man.

683
00:42:35,400 --> 00:42:37,277
''Good luck.
I'm with you all the way.''

684
00:42:37,440 --> 00:42:39,556
All the way from Germany.

685
00:42:39,720 --> 00:42:41,950
Well, you knew he wouldn't be here.

686
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:45,353
I guess he figures
we got it under control.

687
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:50,033
Who are you looking for?

688
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:54,194
If who I'm looking for
comes through that door,

689
00:42:54,360 --> 00:42:57,113
a lot of your problems are over.

690
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:00,835
So after Colonel McRea relieved me
at the supply depot,

691
00:43:01,000 --> 00:43:02,752
I headed back to my car
to drive home.

692
00:43:02,920 --> 00:43:06,230
And as I passed the officers' club,
I heard this door slam.

693
00:43:06,400 --> 00:43:10,188
That's when I saw Private Hughes
come out of the building and run away.

694
00:43:10,360 --> 00:43:11,679
What time was that?

695
00:43:11,840 --> 00:43:13,319
23:00 hours, sir.

696
00:43:13,480 --> 00:43:17,359
Which, as we've already heard,
is approximately the time

697
00:43:17,520 --> 00:43:19,397
that Lieutenant Walters was killed.

698
00:43:20,560 --> 00:43:22,073
Thank you, Sergeant Owens.

699
00:43:22,240 --> 00:43:24,196
Mr. Matlock, do you wish
to question the witness?

700
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:27,397
Yes, sir. Before I do,
I'd like to clear up a matter

701
00:43:27,560 --> 00:43:29,915
concerning a witness
I had planned to call.

702
00:43:30,080 --> 00:43:32,674
I'd like to request a short recess.

703
00:43:33,880 --> 00:43:37,873
Very well. This hearing will reconvene
at 1 0:00 hours.

704
00:43:38,600 --> 00:43:40,192
Hang on.

705
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:51,468
Sergeant Parker,
why aren't you at the hearing?

706
00:43:51,640 --> 00:43:52,675
I changed my mind.

707
00:43:52,840 --> 00:43:55,149
What the hell do you mean
you changed your mind?

708
00:43:55,320 --> 00:43:58,232
Didn't feel like sticking my neck out
for some boy I didn't even know.

709
00:43:58,400 --> 00:44:00,072
Nobody's asking you
to stick your neck out.

710
00:44:00,240 --> 00:44:01,468
I want you to tell the truth.

711
00:44:01,920 --> 00:44:06,550
Yeah, well, the truth is
if Owens is lying, who's he lying for?

712
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:07,709
The brass.

713
00:44:07,880 --> 00:44:09,836
It's your duty to tell the truth.

714
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:12,036
You're forgetting the first rule
of this man's army:

715
00:44:12,200 --> 00:44:13,679
Don't volunteer.

716
00:44:13,840 --> 00:44:15,512
I can subpoena you if I have to.

717
00:44:15,680 --> 00:44:17,636
Yeah, well,
why don't you just do that?

718
00:44:17,800 --> 00:44:19,313
But just remember one thing:

719
00:44:19,480 --> 00:44:22,278
My memory gets real bad real fast.

720
00:44:22,440 --> 00:44:23,839
You know, after this court-martial,

721
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:25,672
you get to pack up
and go home, but me?

722
00:44:26,360 --> 00:44:30,592
I'm a 20-year man. I get to stay here,
and with the officers, so no, thanks.

723
00:44:36,520 --> 00:44:39,671
They're gonna get away
with actual murder,

724
00:44:39,840 --> 00:44:42,991
and they're gonna ruin
an innocent boy's life.

725
00:44:43,440 --> 00:44:45,476
Maybe you don't know
Chris Hughes,

726
00:44:45,640 --> 00:44:47,278
but the next time--

727
00:44:47,440 --> 00:44:50,318
And if you don't testify,
there will be a next time.

728
00:44:50,480 --> 00:44:53,756
--do you know who that poor boy
might just turn out to be?

729
00:44:53,920 --> 00:44:55,399
You.

730
00:44:55,560 --> 00:44:57,790
Now, come on, sergeant.
What do you say?

731
00:45:02,600 --> 00:45:04,477
Be just a minute.

732
00:45:08,160 --> 00:45:10,435
Well, Mr. Matlock,
are you ready to proceed?

733
00:45:10,600 --> 00:45:12,352
Yes, sir.
As soon as my client gets here.

734
00:45:12,520 --> 00:45:13,873
That could be a long time.

735
00:45:14,040 --> 00:45:15,473
Excuse me, sir.

736
00:45:15,640 --> 00:45:17,949
We've searched the post
and found no sign of Private Hughes

737
00:45:18,120 --> 00:45:21,669
or his belongings.
He's AWOL, possibly a deserter.

738
00:45:21,840 --> 00:45:25,037
I've taken steps to have
a warrant issued for his arrest.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
